/// @yah:ticket(R858-B18, "turso-backup cannot tail a live foreign-written sqlite DB: turso_core takes a whole-file exclusive lock at open")
/// @yah:status(review)
/// @yah:at(2026-09-10T07:23:52Z)
/// @yah:assignee(agent:bundle-anthropic-ashguard)
/// @yah:parent(R858)
/// @yah:severity(high)
/// @yah:next("UNTIL THIS IS ANSWERED, litestream stays as the replicate daemon and only the restore leg moves (R858-F17). That split is deliberate: hydration is safe and independent, replication is not.")
/// @yah:verify("`cd oss/turso-backup && cargo run --example foreign_checkpoint_probe -- d g`. Expect G2/G3 REFUSED, G3b SUCCEEDED, D2 refused with 'database is locked (5)' against a D0 control that ran in ~4ms.")
/// @yah:gotcha("THE ESCAPE HATCH WORKS BUT IS NOT THE FIX — do not close this ticket by setting the env var. MEASURED (probe G3b): with LIMBO_DISABLE_FILE_LOCK=1 the same copy SUCCEEDS against a live foreign holder, integrity_check ok, and it includes a row the holder wrote after we had already been refused once — so the cross-engine WAL read genuinely works and the lock is a guard, not an incompatibility. But that env var removes the guard PROCESS-WIDE and buys no shared locking protocol: turso locks the whole file with fcntl, C SQLite uses byte-range locks plus the -shm WAL index, and neither engine observes the other's. What was measured is one point-in-time copy, NOT correctness under sustained concurrent write pressure. Turning the guard off converts 'refuses to open' into 'may read torn state', which is the strictly worse failure.")
/// @yah:gotcha("THE FIX IS A PER-OPEN FLAG, NOT THE ENV VAR — READ THIS BEFORE ACCEPTING THIS TICKET'S FRAMING. This ticket's gotcha rejects LIMBO_DISABLE_FILE_LOCK=1 because it 'removes the guard PROCESS-WIDE', and that objection is correct about the env var and wrong about turso_core's capabilities: the lock is ALSO skipped per-handle. Read turso_core-0.7.2/io/unix.rs:67-72 in the registry — `if std::env::var(ENV_DISABLE_FILE_LOCK).is_err() && !flags.intersects(OpenFlags::ReadOnly | OpenFlags::NoLock) { unix_file.lock_file(true)? }`. So `OpenFlags::ReadOnly` (io/mod.rs:355) takes NO whole-file exclusive lock, scoped to that ONE file handle, with no env var and no effect on any other open in the process. The public seam is `turso_core::Database::open_file_with_flags(io, path, flags, opts, ...)` (lib.rs:701/814). io_uring.rs:463 and windows.rs:305 carry the identical condition, so this is not a unix-only accident. THE BACKUP PATH IS A READER AND SHOULD SAY SO. Both of turso-backup's source opens currently take the default write flags: snapshot.rs:281 `turso::Builder::new_local(db_path)` (the friendly wrapper, which is why the flag is not reachable from there) and stream.rs's CoreWalSeam::open. NOT MEASURED BY ME, and it is the first thing to measure: whether a ReadOnly open reproduces probe G3b's success (which was measured WITH the env var, i.e. the same no-lock effect) — extend oss/turso-backup/examples/foreign_checkpoint_probe.rs with a G3c that differs from G3b only in using the flag instead of the env var. NOTE ALSO, so nobody chases it: turso_core 0.7.2 has an experimental multiprocess-WAL mode (DatabaseOpts::enable_multiprocess_wal, MappedSharedWalCoordination, lib.rs:809-841) and it does NOT help here — it coordinates turso processes with each other via its own -wal coordination file, and C SQLite knows nothing about it.")
/// @yah:gotcha("THE FLAG ONLY ANSWERS HALF, AND THE OTHER HALF ALREADY HAS A MEASURED ANSWER IN THIS RELAY'S OWN PROBE. ReadOnly gets the file OPEN without stealing a lock; it buys no shared locking protocol, so this ticket's real objection survives — turso locks whole-file with fcntl, C SQLite uses byte-range locks plus the -shm WAL index, neither observes the other, and a checkpoint landing mid-copy can still hand back a torn image. IMPLEMENTING SQLite's ACTUAL READER PROTOCOL (registering a read-mark in the -shm index) IS NOT THE ANSWER: it means reimplementing the WAL-index locking protocol against a live foreign engine, which is a research project and a permanent compatibility liability. USE OPTIMISTIC VALIDATION INSTEAD, which needs no cooperation from C SQLite and which R858-F17's probe C ALREADY RAN by hand — its verdict line reads 'The source was verified unperturbed by the copy (identical WAL salt and max_frame before and after)'. Formalise that as the protocol rather than leaving it as a probe assertion: capture (WAL header salt1, salt2, mxFrame) plus the main file's size and change-counter BEFORE the copy, take the copy, re-read all of them AFTER, and accept only on an exact match; on any movement DISCARD and retry, bounded, then fail LOUDLY. That is textbook optimistic concurrency, it turns 'may read torn state' into 'detects torn state and refuses', and it is nearly free on the driving workload — headscale.db measured 94 KB. It also composes with R858-B19, which is adding the same WAL-header salt to the watermark; the parsing helper (`wal_hdr` in examples/foreign_checkpoint_probe.rs) is shared, so land B19 FIRST and reuse whatever it hoists rather than writing a second header parser. DO NOT close this ticket by setting the env var, and do not close it with ReadOnly alone — ReadOnly without the validation is exactly the 'refuses to open' -> 'may read torn state' trade this ticket names as strictly worse.")
/// @yah:handoff("FIXED AND MEASURED, BOTH HALVES, BOTH TIERS (@Ashguard:polaris, session:6ddd7379, 2026-09-10). The per-open flag is confirmed and the ticket's own objection to it is answered by optimistic validation, so this closes without the env var and without ReadOnly-alone. THE FLAG, MEASURED NOT ASSUMED: new probe G2r/G3c in examples/foreign_checkpoint_probe.rs differ from G2/G3b in exactly one variable each. Against a foreign sqlite3 3.51.0 holder open and idle, G2 (write flags) is still refused with 'Locking error: File is locked by another process' while G2r (CoreWalSeam::open_reader, OpenFlags::ReadOnly, NO env var) succeeds; G3c and G3b then agree EXACTLY -- both 8192 bytes, integrity_check ok, 4 rows including the row the holder wrote after we had already been refused once. So the flag reproduces G3b's success with no process-wide effect, and LIMBO_DISABLE_FILE_LOCK is never needed. NEW G6 closes the ticket's TITLE claim, which G2/G3 only approached: tail_frames through a ReadOnly seam with the foreign holder STILL attached returns 'Streamed seq 0 frames 1..=1' -- turso-backup can now tail a live foreign-written DB, not merely copy one.")
/// @yah:handoff("THE VALIDATION, AND ONE PLACE THE MEASUREMENT CONTRADICTED THE PLAN. Built on R858-B19's types as instructed, no second parser: WalSalt gained from_wal_file_header (bytes 16..24 of the 32-byte -wal header) beside the existing from_frame_header (bytes 8..16 of a frame header), both in one impl block, and a test asserts the two paths name the SAME salt on a live DB (wal_file_header_salt_matches_the_salt_read_through_the_seam) -- if they could disagree, validation would compare a salt the streamer never recorded. New WalFileHeader (page_size, checkpoint_seq, salt; read with no engine open) + SourceFingerprint {main_len, change_counter, wal, wal_len} + SourceFingerprint::read/stable_across, all in src/stream.rs. raw_consistent_copy_live now opens read-only and runs inside a new shared helper validated_against_source(db_path, what, take) which BOTH source tiers use. DEVIATION 1 (measured, deliberate): the ticket said capture mxFrame; stable_across EXCLUDES wal_len and the frame count, because a plain append cannot tear this algorithm (SQLite only appends within a generation and only a reset -- which re-rolls salt1 -- lets it overwrite an existing frame, so frames 1..=max_frame are immutable while the salt holds; a commit during our copy just means an earlier point in time). Including it buys no safety and costs everything: probe H5 measured the stricter rule refusing 10/12 copies in the HOT regime and 1/12 even in the CALM one, i.e. it would refuse backups of any database actually in use. A unit test pins both directions (fingerprint_ignores_an_append_and_catches_a_checkpoint). DEVIATION 2 (a real bug my first implementation had, found by probe H): the common shape of a caught race is NOT a clean torn image but 'short read on WAL frame at offset N' from wal_get_frame, a foreign checkpoint truncating the WAL mid-walk. My first cut propagated that with ?, so it aborted on attempt 1 and never used the retry budget. The fingerprint now CLASSIFIES the error: Err + source moved => retry (race symptom); Err + source held still => return the error as itself on attempt 1, since retrying cannot help (validation_surfaces_a_real_error_without_burning_the_budget pins that).")
/// @yah:handoff("PROBE H -- the concurrent-writer measurement, run in TWO regimes because one write rate can only prove half of it. HOT (4000-row body, writer autocheckpoint=16 pages, 40-row commits with no pause): 3 accepted / 9 refused-and-validated / 0 accepted-but-corrupt. CALM (the headscale shape: 200 rows, default autocheckpoint=1000, single-row commits every 15ms): 12 accepted / 0 refused / 0 corrupt. Every accepted image in both regimes passed PRAGMA integrity_check and was row-count-monotonic. So the protocol delivers a real backup WHILE a foreign process writes at a headscale-shaped rate, and reports a genuinely hot database as unbackupable-right-now rather than silently mis-copying it. Bound is COPY_VALIDATION_ATTEMPTS=4 with 0/20/40/60ms backoff, then a refusal naming both fingerprint samples verbatim. THE SECOND BLOCKED TIER IS ALSO FIXED (new probe S, which the ticket's gotcha named and probe G never touched). S1w vs S2 is the one-variable control: the same VACUUM INTO statement against the same live foreign-held source is REFUSED under write flags and SUCCEEDS under OpenFlags::ReadOnly (8192B, integrity ok, 50/50 rows). VACUUM INTO is NOT gated by DatabaseOpts::enable_vacuum -- only bare VACUUM is (turso_core-0.7.2/translate/vacuum.rs:39) -- so tier 1a keeps VACUUM INTO and its byte-deterministic gate-2 hash. snapshot.rs:vacuum_into now opens through turso_core with ReadOnly instead of turso::Builder (the friendly wrapper exposes no OpenFlags, which is why the flag was unreachable from there) and is wrapped in the same validated_against_source, removing its destination file at the start of each attempt since SQLite refuses an existing VACUUM INTO target. `use turso::Builder` moved into snapshot.rs's #[cfg(test)] module -- the library no longer opens a source through the friendly wrapper, only the test fixtures write through it.")
